# Codex Adversarial Review

Target: branch diff against origin/main
Verdict: needs-attention

No-ship. The design embeds a deadlocking temporal contract, under-constrains both lifecycle models, and leaves purge/redaction safety dependent on future caller discipline.

Findings:
- [high] Failure-first closure creates an ABBA deadlock and can permanently record failure as success (src/lib/solicitation/outreach.ts:219-230)
  The documented contract requires callers to fail the send parent before closing. That takes the agent_runs lock first; closeBatchIfSettled takes the batch lock first and later locks agent_runs through recordStep/completeRun. A concurrent backstop or last-child close therefore forms run→batch versus batch→run. If PostgreSQL aborts the failure transaction, the closer can observe no error code, CAS send_closed_at, and record exhausted/completed. A retry cannot repair it because both closure and terminal run transitions subsequently no-op. This also disproves the NON_REVIEW_SUBJECT_CALLER justification that no run-lock holder waits on solicitation_batches.
  Recommendation: Make batch→run the sole lock order. Replace the temporal contract with one operation that accepts the explicit failure/disposition, locks the batch first, records the parent error, closes the batch, and terminalizes the parent atomically.
- [high] The advisory lock does not protect an in-flight provider send from purge (worker/jobs/account-offboarding/purge-stages.ts:270-309)
  The purge takes the transaction-scoped advisory lock and then unconditionally deletes contact_csv_uploads, cascading through batches and outreach. Once #630 commits delivery_status='sending', its advisory lock is released before the provider call; purge can then acquire the lock and delete the committed sending row while that call is in flight. This is a real send-with-no-record gap, not solved by the advertised global lock order. The ADR assigns a future fix, but this primitive is unsafe by default for its intended consumer.
  Recommendation: After acquiring all advisory locks, have purge detect committed sending claims and durably defer/fail the purge until they resolve. Add a live race test covering claim commit, paused provider call, purge refusal, and terminal completion.
- [high] Timestamp-only batch state accepts impossible and unsafe lifecycle histories (supabase/migrations/091_send_mode_spine_lifecycle.sql:142-159)
  The ordering CHECK only requires sending_started_at to imply approval and closure to imply built. It permits approved_at on an unbuilt batch, approval before built_at, an orphan veto_deadline_at, and closure timestamps that precede earlier lifecycle events. deriveBatchState then masks these contradictions using precedence; for example, approved_at with built_at=NULL is reported as approved and enters the one-active-send index. A future approval bug could therefore approve content that does not yet exist and later release messages the owner never reviewed.
  Recommendation: Keep timestamp derivation only if the database enforces the complete transition graph: implication and chronological CHECKs for build, approval, veto, sending, closure, and halt, plus centralized CAS transition functions. Otherwise use one authoritative status/disposition with timestamps as history.
- [high] Outreach status and lifecycle timestamps can contradict each other (supabase/migrations/091_send_mode_spine_lifecycle.sql:260-315)
  delivery_status is constrained only against disposition_reason, not queued_at/sending_at/sent_at. A legal status='sent', sent_at=NULL row retains its body forever because the redaction sweep never selects it. Conversely, a legal live status='rendered' row with an old sent_at is redacted into a bodyless live attempt, leaving the batch unable to send or settle. The schema therefore has two uncoordinated owners for delivery state.
  Recommendation: Add a total status-to-timestamp consistency and ordering constraint, with integration tests proving sent rows must have sent_at and non-sent live rows cannot have sent_at or body_redacted_at.
- [medium] The 90-day redaction leaves source PII indefinitely readable (worker/background/pollers/ArchivalPoller.ts:251-265)
  The sweep nulls only rendered_subject and rendered_body. interpolation_inputs remains on the retained row forever and is owner-readable; its specified shape contains contact_first_name and visit_note. Redacting the rendered prose therefore does not fulfill the stated data-minimization objective for the source personal data captured specifically for that message.
  Recommendation: Redact interpolation_inputs with the body, or split reproducibility data into a time-limited sensitive payload and retain only hashes, versions, and non-PII classifications after 90 days.

Next steps:
- Redesign closure so disposition and parent failure are owned by one batch-first transaction.
- Make purge safely defer on durable in-flight send claims before #630 can consume this seam.
- Add database constraints for valid batch and outreach lifecycle histories.
- Extend retention tests to verify all message-specific PII is removed after 90 days.
